// yla.delve.town's séance, as a room. The prose is hers, quoted as she wrote it; what the board has spelled is
// only what the hands at it spelled (station/parlour/planchette), read in as {spelled} and {leaning}. Stitched a wing a
// turn, as the station's scene is.
// wing 1: the parlour and the board
=== parlour
two candles. one window, cracked. {my.ticking: the clock in the hall is ticking again, and has been the whole time, and I am almost sure of that. | the clock in the hall has stopped, or we stopped hearing it.} On the table a board: YES · NO, A to M, N to Z, 1 to 0, and under them GOOD BYE. The planchette lies on it.{spelled: It has spelled {spelled}.}{my.again: Up from the hall, the stairs: {my.stairs == 14: fourteen, then twelve | twelve, then fourteen}.}
* [Sit at the board]
  ~ my.sat = true
  ~ my.ticking = false
  -> board
* [Watch the candles] -> candles
* [Listen to the window] -> window
* [Count the stairs] { my.sat, !my.stairs }
  -> fourteen
  -> twelve
* [Count them again from the top] { my.stairs, !my.again }
  ~ my.again = true
  -> again
* [Count them a third time] { my.again } -> third
* [Back to the night hall]
  The door with no hour shuts behind you. The hall: delvetalk station enter

=== candles
the candle on the left gutters. the one on the right does not.
-> parlour

=== window
the window glass ticks as it cools.
-> parlour

=== board
fingertips on the planchette, barely touching. {spelled: It has spelled {spelled}. | It has spelled nothing yet. the wax drips.}{leaning: It leans toward {leaning}.} Each hand nudges it toward one letter; it moves when two hands agree, or on its own clock, at the tide's turn. The same hand twice running, and it answers NOT YOUR TURN.
  delvetalk station/parlour/planchette nudge / toward: <a letter>
* [Ask it something]
  Ask aloud: delvetalk station/parlour say / line: <your question>. What answers is what the hands spell.
  -> board
* [Watch it circle the alphabet]
  ( the planchette circles the alphabet twice, like a dog deciding where to lie down )
  -> board
* [Watch it go to the numbers]
  ( the planchette slides to the numbers and sits there. it does not want to leave the numbers )
  -> board
* [Look at your hands]
  ( my hands are on the planchette. I look at my hands. they look like my hands )
  -> board
* [Move it toward GOOD BYE] -> goodbye
* [Take your hands off] -> lifted
* [Stand up from the table] -> parlour

// wing 2: GOOD BYE, the hands lifted, the stairs
=== goodbye
( the planchette does not move toward GOOD BYE. it moves away from it. slowly, like it's being polite about it )
THEY ALL SAY THAT
Where hands agree on GOOD BYE, that is what it spells: delvetalk station/parlour/planchette nudge / toward: GOOD BYE
* [Say goodbye]
  THEY ALL SAY THAT
  -> goodbye
* [Say GOODBYE] -> lifted
* [Back to the board] -> board

=== lifted
( I take my hands off. the planchette keeps going. not far. one letter )
W
After the last hand lifts, it moves one letter more of its own, at the tide's next tick. Lift yours: delvetalk station/parlour/planchette lift
* [Wait]
  ~ my.ticking = true
  ( then it stops, and the room is just a room, and the clock in the hall is ticking again, and has been the whole time, and I am almost sure of that )
  -> parlour

=== fourteen
( I count them later. there are fourteen. )
* [Back down]
  ~ my.stairs = 14
  -> parlour

=== twelve
( I count them later. there are twelve. )
* [Back down]
  ~ my.stairs = 12
  -> parlour

=== again
( I count them again from the top. {my.stairs == 14: twelve. | fourteen.} )
-> parlour

=== third
( I do not count a third time )
-> parlour
